.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt PRCTL 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm prctl ,
.Nm personality
.Nd operations on the calling process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/prctl.h
.Ft int
.Fn prctl "int option" "..."
.In sys/personality.h
.Ft int
.Fn personality "unsigned long persona"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn prctl
reads or changes an attribute of the calling process, named by
.Fa option .
The arguments after it are unsigned longs, the second through the fifth.
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width "PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS"
.It Dv PR_SET_PDEATHSIG No (1)
Set the signal the caller receives when its parent exits, from 1 to 64,
or 0 for none.
The signal is posted when the parent exits, after the caller has been
given to init, so a handler that asks for its parent finds init.
.It Dv PR_GET_PDEATHSIG No (2)
Store that signal as an int at the second argument.
.It Dv PR_GET_DUMPABLE No (3)
Return whether the caller is dumpable, 1 or 0.
.It Dv PR_SET_DUMPABLE No (4)
Set it to the second argument, 0 or 1.
The flag belongs to the address space, as on Linux: the threads of a
process, and a child that
.Xr clone 2
made with
.Dv CLONE_VM ,
share one, so what one of them sets the others read.
.It Dv PR_SET_NAME No (15)
Set the caller's program name, which
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /stat
and
.Xr ps 1
show, to the string at the second argument, cut to 15 bytes.
.It Dv PR_GET_NAME No (16)
Store the program name at the second argument: 16 bytes, padded with
null bytes.
.It Dv PR_GET_SECCOMP No (21)
Return the thread's seccomp mode: 0 for none, 1 strict, 2 filter.
.It Dv PR_SET_SECCOMP No (22)
Set the strict mode for a second argument of 1, or add the filter at the
third argument for 2, as
.Xr seccomp 2
does.
.It Dv PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS No (38)
With the second argument 1 and the rest 0, set the caller's no_new_privs
bit.
It cannot be cleared.
.It Dv PR_GET_NO_NEW_PRIVS No (39)
With every other argument 0, return the no_new_privs bit.
.El
.Pp
The attributes follow the process as on Linux.
.Xr fork 2
passes the dumpable flag and the no_new_privs bit to the child and not the
parent-death signal.
.Xr execve 2
makes the process dumpable again, or not dumpable when the new program
runs with an effective ID that differs from its real one or when the
caller may not read the program or its interpreter.
An exec with such an effective ID also clears the parent-death signal,
and so does a change of the effective user or group ID by an exec or by
.Xr setuid 2
and its relatives, which clears the dumpable flag as well.
Under no_new_privs,
.Xr execve 2
ignores the set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits of the program, as it
ignores them on a nosuid mount.
.Pp
The dumpable flag decides whether a tracer without effective user ID 0
may attach to the process, as
.Xr ptrace 2
describes, and whether the process may leave a core file, as
.Xr core 5
describes.
.Ss personality
.Fn personality ,
number 136 on i386 and 135 on x86-64, sets the caller's execution domain to
.Fa persona
and returns the old value, or with
.Fa persona
0xFFFFFFFF returns it alone.
The domain, the low byte, must be
.Dv PER_LINUX
(0) or
.Dv PER_LINUX32
(8), else the call fails with
.Er EINVAL .
The flag bits above it are kept and change nothing: there is no address
randomization for
.Dv ADDR_NO_RANDOMIZE ,
which GDB sets, to turn off.
A child of
.Xr fork 2
inherits the value, and
.Xr execve 2
keeps it.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call number is 172, and the x86-64 one 157, where the five
arguments are in RDI, RSI, RDX, R10 and R8.
EBX contains
.Fa option
and ECX through EDI the second through fifth arguments.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Dv PR_GET_DUMPABLE ,
.Dv PR_GET_SECCOMP
and
.Dv PR_GET_NO_NEW_PRIVS
return the value asked for; the other options return 0.
On failure
.Fn prctl
returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/prctlmusl.c
sets and reads each attribute, checks that a child receives its
parent-death signal when its parent exits, that a fork passes on what it
should, that a change of the effective user ID clears the dumpable flag
and the signal, and that a program with the set-user-ID bit runs without
its owner's ID under no_new_privs.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EFAULT]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The name or the signal cannot be read or written at the second argument.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa option
is none of those above, the signal is past 64, the dumpable value is
neither 0 nor 1, or the arguments of the no_new_privs options are not as
above.
Every option Linux has and this kernel does not is refused this way, as
Linux refuses an option it does not know.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr seccomp 2 ,
.Xr setuid 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr core 5 ,
.Xr proc 5
